 i see you\'re here for back pain .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] hey dragon , i\'m seeing mrs. lee today for , uh , back pain . she\'s a 40-year-old female . so can you tell me what happened to you ?\n[patient] yes . i was at a birthday party and it was surprise party . so we were all like crunched behind and hiding . and when the person came in we all jumped out , and then i fell down the little stair and i rammed my back into the railing of the s- , uh , staircase .\n[doctor] so it was a surprise for you too ?\n[patient] yes it was .\n[doctor] very good . okay . so , um , did it hurt because you hit it , or did you twist ? what happened when you ?\n[patient] i think it was my motion-\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] . of jumping up , or something .\n[doctor] okay .\n[patient] and maybe a twisting .\n[doctor] okay . any pain shooting down your legs at all ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] is it on one side or the other of your back ?\n[patient] um , it\'s more on the right .\n[doctor] on the right . is there anything that makes it better or worse ?\n[patient] uh , if i\'m lying flat it hurts a little more , and any twisting motion for sure .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and do you have any history or any back problems before ?\n[patient] i did . i had a fusion done .\n[doctor] okay , yeah that lumbar fusion . um , let\'s take an x-ray . hey . or let\'s look at an x-ray . hey dragon , show me the latest x-ray . okay , we can see where you had your fusion , t4 here on the x-ray . but i do n\'t see anything that looks , um , significantly abnormal . it does n\'t appear to have any compression fractures or anything like that . so , it\'s essentially , except for the fusion , a normal exam . let me do your exam for you here . can i have you stand up please ? does it hurt when i twist you that way ?\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . you can sit back down . if i lift your leg here , does that bother you ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] how \'bout when i lift this leg ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any pain shooting down your legs when i do that ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] okay . and can you feel me touching down here .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay , great . so , you had a injury to your back when you were twisting at the birthday party . you do n\'t really have any pain or numbness down your legs . you have some stiffness with rotation , um , but you do n\'t have any evidence of a , um , what we would call a pinched nerved or radiculopathy . so , i think you basically have a strain of your lower back . um , have you been taking any medications ?\n[patient] just some ibuprofen .\n[doctor] okay . and do you have any other , um , medical problems ?\n[patient] um i take medication for anxiety .\n[doctor] okay . and that\'s all ? okay . um , so what we\'re gon na do is i\'m gon na give you , um , just continue the motrin at 800mg , three times a day , with food . i want you to use some ice and heat , alternating on your back . but i do want you walking .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , and , uh , this should sort of take care of itself after a few days . if it\'s not , or it\'s getting worse , i want you to come back and see me over the next week .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] does that sound good ?\n[patient] it does .\n[doctor] okay , dragon . hey dragon , go ahead and order the medications and procedures and close out the note . come on i\'ll bring you out to check out .\n[patient] thank you .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of lumbar fusion.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports she was injured at a birthday party where she was jumping and twisting.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports pain in her back.\n• Neurological: Denies pain or numbness in her legs.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck: Supine exam: No focal tenderness. Extension, flexion, and rotation are normal. Supine exam: No focal tenderness. Extension, flexion, and rotation are normal.\n• Back: Supine exam: No focal tenderness. Extension, flexion, and rotation are normal. Supine exam: No focal tenderness. Extension, flexion, and rotation are normal.\n• Extremities: No focal tenderness. Strength is normal. Reflexes are normal.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the spine shows a fusion at T4, otherwise normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Strain of the lower back.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is a 40-year-old female who presents with a strain of her lower back after a twisting injury at a birthday party. She has a history of a lumbar fusion. There is no focal tenderness or pain on palpation of her neck or back. She denies pain or numbness in her legs. X-ray of the spine shows a fusion at T4, otherwise normal. She will continue taking 800mg Motrin three times a day with food. She will use ice and heat alternating on her back. I encourage her to walk. If she does not improve or if it worsens, she will follow up with me in 1 week.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue taking 800mg Motrin three times a day with food. Use ice and heat alternating on her back. Encourage her to walk. If she does not improve or if it worsens, she will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. She reports she was at a birthday party and it was a surprise. She states that she was hiding behind someone and when the person came in, she jumped out and she fell down the stairs and hit her back on the railing of the stairs. She states that she is unsure if she twisted at the time of the injury. She denies any pain down her legs. She states that she had lumbar fusion at T4. She denies any numbness or tingling in her legs.\n\nThe patient states that she is having pain on the right side of her back. She states that the pain is worse with twisting and lying flat. She states that she is taking ibuprofen 200mg three times a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her legs.\n\nVITALS\n\n• BP: 124/72. No orthostatic changes.\n• HR: 64. Regular rate and rhythm.\n• RR: 16. No orthopnea or dyspnea.\n• Temp: 98.6.\n• Saturation: 99% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n• Gastrointestinal: Bowel sounds are present.\n• Musculoskeletal: No pain with palpation of the lower back. No focal tenderness to palpation. No pain with straight leg raising or extension. Positive pain with rotation. No focal weakness or atrophy.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbosacral spine.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Back pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient will continue to take ibuprofen 800mg three times a day with food. I advised her to use ice and heat, alternating on her back. I advised her to ambulate. She will follow up with me in one week if she is not improving or worsening.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient was advised to continue to take ibuprofen 800mg three times a day with food. She was advised to use ice and heat, alternating on her back. She was advised to ambulate. She will follow up with me in one week if she is not improving or worsening. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCC:\n\nBack pain.\n\nHPI:\n\nMs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for an evaluation of back pain. She states she was at a birthday party and she ran her back into the railing of the staircase and twisted her back. She denies any pain radiating down her legs. Lying flat and twisting motions aggravate the pain. She is currently taking ibuprofen.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS:\n\nIbuprofen.\n\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\n\nAnxiety.\n\nPAST SURGICAL HISTORY:\n\nLumbar fusion\n\nEXAM\n\nExamination of the back shows range of motion without pain. Straight leg raise is negative. Sensation is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine show no obvious signs of acute fracture. Evidence of a prior lumbar fusion with hardware in good position.\n\nIMPRESSION\n\nLumbar spine strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAt this point, I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. I have recommended ice and heat. She will continue with Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She will follow up with me as needed.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


