y seen in the emergency room with high blood pressure and palpitations. He states that he was having some coffee and no breakfast at that time, and he believes that was a panic attack rather than a hypertensive emergency. He denies having palpitations since that time.\n\nThe patient reports that he has a history of hypertension. He denies a family history of hypertension. He states that he was recently placed on Benicar HCT, 40-25, and he is also taking Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He reports that he is checking his blood pressure daily at home and his readings are between 146-155.\n\nThe patient reports that he is trying to cut back on his salt intake. He states that he has been walking on the treadmill when he can. He denies any other medical problems.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Benicar HCT, 40-25\n• Amlodipine, 10 mg daily\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 146/55 mmHg\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm without murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nThyroid profile from 3/1/2021: Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) 2.1 mcU/mL. Free thyroxine (FT4) 1.2 ng/dL.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nResistant hypertension.\n\nThe patient is a 49-year-old male who presents with hypertension. He is currently taking three medications and has not achieved adequate blood pressure control. He is also taking Benicar HCT, 40-25, and Amlodipine, 10 mg daily. His blood pressure is currently running 146/55 mmHg. He reports that he is checking his blood pressure daily at home and his readings are between 146-155. He denies a family history of hypertension. He is trying to cut back on his salt intake and has been walking on the treadmill when he can.\n\nI have added Spironolactone to his regimen. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in two weeks. If he is not controlled on Spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to resistant hypertension 