hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nChristopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents for follow-up of adult hydrocephalus. He was initially seen in May 2020 for an 8-10 year history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. The patient underwent shunt surgery on August 1, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was placed. The shunt was set at 2.0.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been doing much better since his surgery. He notes that his walking has improved and he is now able to walk to the dining room. He is still having some issues with bladder control, but he has leveled off. He is wearing a diaper and has not had any issues with sores. He has not had any pain or headaches since his surgery.\n\nThe patient\'s daughter reports that she has noticed an improvement in his walking. He is able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which he was unable to do prior to his surgery. She also reports that he is doing better with his bladder control, but has leveled off. She notes that she has to help him at times.\n\nThe patient reports that he is doing better cognitively. He states that he is able to have conversations without feeling lost.\n\nThe patient reports no pain or discomfort where the shunt is located.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient lives with his daughter.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports no fever or unintentional weight loss. Gastrointestinal: Denies weight loss or change in appetite. Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output. Musculoskeletal: Reports walking impairment. Neurological: Reports cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations. Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 124/80. Heart rate: 64. Respirations: 18. Weight