oms. He reports that he has been slowed down a little bit and has had to slow down activities. He has been using a cane to help him get up from a sitting position.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with right knee pain and right leg swelling. The patient\'s right knee pain returned after a steroid injection 2 months ago. The patient\'s right knee pain is likely related to his rheumatoid arthritis. The patient\'s right leg swelling is likely related to right knee pain and right knee swelling.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to continue with his current medications and to follow up with his PCP. He was advised to try compression socks, reduce his salt intake, and prop his feet up at night. He was advised to make an appointment with his PCP for blood work in the future.\n\nMedical Treatment: The patient received a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection today. The risks and benefits and alternatives were explained. The patient was advised of the risks of the steroid injection, including, but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wear