ght knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen 2 months ago for a right knee injection.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he wears compression socks but they have not been helpful.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nXeljanz 11mg daily, Celebrex 200mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary incontinence.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nSkin: Reports skin lesions on his legs.\nNeurological: Denies headaches.\nPsychiatric: Denies depression.\nEndocrine: Denies diabetes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right lower extremity reveals normal capillary refill in the toes. The right knee is swollen, with pitting edema. Range of motion is slightly diminished. The knee is hot to touch. \n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPatient reports right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6 out of 10. The pain has been present for 2 months. The right knee is swollen, with pitting edema. The knee is hot to touch. Range of motion is slightly diminished. \n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient reports right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6 out of 10. The pain has been present for 2 months. The right knee is swollen, with pitting edema. The knee is hot to touch. Range of motion is slightly diminished. He reports that he was last seen 2 months ago for a right knee injection. The injection provided temporary relief, but the pain has returned. The patient reports that he wears compression socks but they have not been helpful. He is currently on Xeljanz 11mg daily and Celebrex 200mg daily.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that we will give him another steroid injection today. We will also order an x-ray of the right knee to see where the swelling is coming from. We will set up physical therapy and if nothing helps, we will need to get a surgery consult.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. Last seen in clinic 2 months ago for right knee pain. He received a steroid injection at that time. He reports that the injection provided temporary relief for a couple of weeks, but his pain has returned. He is now experiencing pain in his entire leg, which is swollen.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful in alleviating his symptoms. He reports that he has been slowed down a little bit and has had to slow down activities. He has been using a cane to help him get up from a sitting position.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that he is taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nNeurological: Denies headaches.\nSkin: Denies rashes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the right knee reveals slight diminished range of motion. There is pitting around the knee and ankle. Capillary refill is normal in the toes. The knee is warm to touch.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRheumatoid Arthritis.\nRight Knee Pain.\nRight Leg Swelling.\n\nPatient is a 71-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for ongoing management of his rheumatoid arthritis. He reports that his right knee pain has returned after a steroid injection 2 months ago. He is now experiencing pain in his entire leg, which is swollen. The patient reports that he has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful in alleviating his symptoms. He reports that he has been slowed down a little bit and has had to slow down activities. He has been using a cane to help him get up from a sitting position.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms are consistent with right knee pain and right leg swelling. The patient\'s right knee pain returned after a steroid injection 2 months ago. The patient\'s right knee pain is likely related to his rheumatoid arthritis. The patient\'s right leg swelling is likely related to right knee pain and right knee swelling.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: The patient was advised to continue with his current medications and to follow up with his PCP. He was advised to try compression socks, reduce his salt intake, and prop his feet up at night. He was advised to make an appointment with his PCP for blood work in the future.\n\nMedical Treatment: The patient received a right knee fluid aspiration and steroid injection today. The risks and benefits and alternatives were explained. The patient was advised of the risks of the steroid injection, including, but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Nelson is a 72-year-old male who presents today for ongoing management of rheumatoid arthritis. He was last seen by me on 03/04/2021, where he received a steroid injection to his right knee. He continues to experience right knee pain and swelling. The pain level is 6/10. He notes when he stands after he has been sitting for a while, he has to use a cane to "get his bearings." The injection he received to the right knee at the last visit was only beneficial for a few weeks. The patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
