re is no ecchymosis, edema, or erythema noted. Tenderness to palpation of the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right wrist.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow: No fracture or bony abnormalities noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient presents with right elbow pain that is consistent with medial epicondylitis. X-ray of the right elbow reveals no fracture or bony abnormalities.\n   Medical Treatment: Prescribed sling for right arm. Apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. Take 600 mg of ibuprofen, 3 times a day, for 1 week. Hold off on pottery for the next 2 weeks.\n2. Hypertension.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently controlled on lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n   Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n3. Diabetes.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently controlled on metformin 500 mg twice a day. Patient has difficulty keeping up with daily blood glucose monitoring.\n   Medical Treatment: Increase metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. Patient to check blood glucose daily and record. Referral to diabetic counseling.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient presents with right elbow pain that is consistent with medial epicondylitis. X-ray of the right elbow reveals no fracture or bony abnormalities.\n   Medical Treatment: Prescribed sling for right arm. Apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. Take 600 mg of ibuprofen, 3 times a day, for 1 week. Hold off on pottery for the next 2 weeks.\n2. Hypertension.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently controlled on lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n   Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n3. Diabetes.\n   Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently controlled on metformin 500 mg twice a day. Patient has difficulty keeping up with daily blood glucose monitoring.\n   Medical Treatment: Increase metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. Patient to check 