tient does have a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse okay well let\'s just i wan na talk a little bit about i had the nurse do an x-ray on you and i looked at the results of your knee x-ray and it does n\'t show any acute fracture or bony abnormality which is not surprising based on your injury so let\'s just talk a little bit about you know my assessment and my plan for you so i think you do have a a lateral a lateral collateral ligament strain based on your exam findings and this this type of injury essentially can be healed by itself you know i do n\'t think we\'re gon na need to refer you to surgery or anything like that i want you to go ahead and i\'m gon na prescribe meloxicam fifteen milligrams once a day i do wan na go ahead and refer you to physical therapy because if we strengthen up those muscles and areas around that injury then that will make your knee stronger it help prevent future injuries are you able to ice it at all during the day now you said you working you work in sales so you said you\'re home\n[patient] yeah i actually i\'m at home so i can ice it and i have been icing it\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i mean twenty on twenty off at least four times a day\n[doctor] okay and so does that help at all\n[patient] it\'s it it makes it feel better in the morning when i first go to my desk and at the end of the day before bed you know i take ibuprofen so i do n\'t have swelling overnight but honestly i have a pretty decent threshold for pain so it\'s not good it\'s not changing it by much\n[doctor] okay do you feel like your symptoms are getting worse or better i think i think pretty much for the last two weeks they\'ve remained about the same the the only time i feel like they get worse is when i exert myself doing up and down like gardening in the yard or moving pots\n[patient] and stuff outside that\'s the only time i felt like it got any worse but nothing to the point where it stayed worse\n[doctor] okay alright well let\'s go ahead and refer you to physical therapy if you\'re still having pain then we might have to consider an mri\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] any questions about that\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay alright well it was very nice seeing you today call me if you need anything\n[patient] i will\n[doctor] okay bye\n[patient] bye\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient is a 44-year-old male with a past medical history significant for a broken right second toe who presents with right knee pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales and is active with cycling. He reports that he is currently riding his bike indoors for the winter. He enjoys rock and regain music rides.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in the right foot.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies any chest pain or palpitations.\n\nVITALS\n\n• BP: 124/74 mmHg\n• HR: 72 bpm\n• RR: 16 breaths per minute\n• Temp: 98.6°F\n• SpO2: 98% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Slight edema of the right knee. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full range of motion. Negative Lachman sign. Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\n• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. S1 and S2 normal. No peripheral edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Right knee x-ray: No acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Lateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with right knee pain and swelling. He reports a history of breaking his right second toe multiple times. He denies any numbness or tingling in the right foot. He reports pain with varus stress of the right knee. He has a positive varus test and a negative Lachman sign. X-ray of the right knee shows no acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n• Medical Treatment: We discussed the diagnosis and treatment plan with the patient today. We discussed that the patient does not need surgery and that we can manage his symptoms with physical therapy and medication. We will refer the patient to physical therapy. I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment plan with the patient today. I explained that he has a lateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee and that he does not need surgery. I advised him to follow up with physical therapy and to take Meloxicam 15 mg once daily. I also advised him to continue icing his knee as tolerated and to avoid activities that exacerbate his symptoms.\n• Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Smith is a pleasant 56-year-old male who presents today with right knee pain. The patient states he injured his right knee 2.5 weeks ago when he stepped off a curb. The patient denies falling down. He states that he initially felt pain in his ankle, but after the ankle pain went away, he noticed that his knee was sore. The patient notes that it clicks when he walks. The pain is worse when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient denies any pain at rest. He has been taking ibuprofen, but notes that it does not help with the pain during the day. The patient states that at night, it does help him sleep better. The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his foot.\n\nThe patient states that he is active and rides a bike. He notes that he does squats and that is when he feels the most clicking. He denies any pain with bending. The patient states that he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times in the last 10 years. He notes that he has some numbness depending on the shoe he wears.\n\nThe patient denies any radicular symptoms.\n\nMEDICAL REASON\n\nThe patient presents today with right knee pain. He reports a 2.5-week history of right knee pain after stepping off a curb. He denies any fall. He reports that he initially felt pain in his ankle, but after the ankle pain went away, he noticed that his knee was sore. He notes that it clicks when he walks. The patient denies any pain at rest. He has been taking ibuprofen, but notes that it does not help with the pain during the day. He states that at night, it does help him sleep better. He denies any numbness or tingling in his foot. The patient reports that he is active and rides a bike. He notes that he does squats and that is when he feels the most clicking. He denies any pain with bending. The patient states that he has broken his second toe on his right foot three times in the last 10 years. He notes that he has some numbness depending on the shoe he wears. The patient denies any radicular symptoms. He reports that his symptoms have remained the same for the past 2 weeks. He states that the only time his symptoms get worse is when he is exerting himself doing up and down movements outside.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee reveals slight edema. There is no pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. The patient has a positive varus test. There is full range of motion. There is a negative Lachman sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee was reviewed and it does not show any acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. I will refer him to physical therapy. I will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day. I advised him to ice his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off 4 times a day. If his symptoms do not improve, we will consider an MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient was advised to ice his knee 20 minutes on and 20 minutes off 4 times a day. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
