ounds in 2 days. He was advised to see a nutritionist for education on dietary restrictions.\n- Medical Treatment: The patient will continue on Bumex 2 mg daily.\n- Follow Up: Follow up in 3 months.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue on Cozaar 100 mg daily and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\n3. Stage 3 Chronic Kidney Disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Treatment: We will obtain a renal artery ultrasound to rule out secondary causes.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute on chronic CHF.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports social drinking and enjoys watching football, but does not smoke.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a family history of hypertension.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking his medications regularly, but admits he may miss a dose or two. He is currently taking bumex 2 mg once daily, cozaar 100 mg once daily, and norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports shortness of breath. Denies chest pain.\n• Cardiovascular: Reports shortness of breath.\n• Respiratory: Reports shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Reports swelling of his ankles. Denies chest pain.\n• Genitourinary: Reports stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nVITALS\n\n• BP: 128/72 mmHg.\n• HR: 79.\n• RR: 26.\n• O2 Sat: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n• Musculoskeletal: 1+ edema in both lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with acute on chronic CHF. He reports dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue bumex 2 mg once daily. Continue dietary restrictions. Weight check daily and call if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days. Patient will see a nutritionist for education on what foods he can eat.\n2. Hypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue cozaar 100 mg once daily. Continue norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n3. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Treatment: Renal artery ultrasound. Further labs.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPatient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral ankle swelling.\nNeurological: Denies any sleep disturbance.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 128/72.\nHR: 79 bpm.\nRR: 26.\nSpO2: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distension noted.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. There is a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: There is trace edema to the bilateral lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPrevious echocardiogram is reviewed and revealed a preserved EF of 55% abnormal diastolic filling and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to be cause by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\