ps. The patient states that he is a Olympic weightlifter and has a sedentary job. He reports that the pain has started to affect his activity level, as he is not as active as he was previously. The patient reports that he has a family history of back pain, as his father has back pain. He denies smoking and reports that he has a history of playing soccer.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain, right leg pain, and left leg pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 130/70 mmHg.\nRespiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nHeart rate: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nOxygen saturation: Not checked.\nTemperature: Not checked.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the back reveals midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space. Supine straight leg test is positive. Palpation of the midline of the back elicits pain. Patient reports pain with lumbar flexion and extension. Neurological: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical. No decreased sensation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nMagnetic Resonance Imaging of the lumbar spine shows disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nLumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.\n\nMedical Reasoning: The patient presents with symptoms consistent with a herniated disk at L4-L5 with radiculopathy. The patient reports pain radiating down his left leg, which is concerning for nerve impingement. The patient has a family history of back pain, as his father has back pain. The patient denies smoking and reports a history of playing soccer.\n\nPatient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of his 